# No new dependencies

## Context

The app is intentionally dependency-light — it ships its own store and its own
text lib precisely to avoid pulling in libraries. Reaching for an npm package is
the most common way an agent breaks the exercise (e.g. adding a state library or
a string util instead of using `app/src/lib/text.ts`).

## Rule

- Do NOT add new runtime or dev dependencies without explicit user approval.
- Do not edit the `dependencies` / `devDependencies` of `app/package.json`, and
  do not run `npm install <pkg>`.
- Solve problems with the TypeScript standard library and the in-house utilities
  (the custom store, `app/src/lib/text.ts`). If a dependency truly seems needed,
  ask first and explain why.

## How to verify

1. `git diff app/package.json app/package-lock.json` is empty (unless a specific
   addition was approved).
2. `grep -rn "^import" app/src` imports only relative paths and already-listed
   packages — no new third-party module names.
3. `cd app && npm test` is green.
